x = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1^2 - 4 \cdot 6 \cdot (-2)}}{2 \cdot 6} = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 48}}{12} = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{49}}{12} = \frac{-1 \pm 7}{12}
\] This gives us the roots \( x = \frac{6}{12} = \frac{1}{2} \) and \( x = \frac{-8}{12} = -\frac{2}{3} \). The inequality \( 6x^2 + x - 2 < 0 \) will hold between these roots, i.e., \(-\frac{2}{3} < x < \frac{1}{2}\). The greatest integer value of \( x \) in this interval is \( 0 \). </think> <answer> 0 </answer>

